.. only:: not html

 * :doc:`/api/gallery/statmech/partition_functions/plot_01_vibrational_heat_capacity`

.. raw:: html

    <div class="sphx-glr-thumbcontainer" tooltip="James Clerk Maxwell (1860) and, in fully general form, Ludwig Boltzmann (1871) showed that every classical quadratic degree of freedom of a system in thermal equilibrium contributes exactly \frac12k_B to its heat capacity -- the equipartition theorem. A rigid diatomic molecule therefore &quot;should&quot; carry \frac32k_B (translation) + k_B (2 rotational degrees of freedom) + k_B (1 vibrational mode, kinetic + potential) = \frac72k_B at every temperature -- a prediction 19th-century equipartition theory could not explain deviations from (Maxwell himself flagged the puzzle sharply in 1879). Only VibrationalPartitionFunctionHarmonic is an exact quantum treatment in this package, so it alone shows the real resolution Einstein gave in 1907: a mode&#x27;s contribution only &quot;switches on&quot; once k_BT exceeds its own quantum spacing. RotationalPartitionFunctionLinear, by contrast, implements only the classical high-temperature limit (as its own docstring flags), so it contributes its full k_B at every temperature here -- a deliberate approximation, harmless in practice because real rotational spacings are tiny compared to vibrational ones (as the numbers below confirm), but worth seeing explicitly rather than assuming.">

.. only:: html

  .. image:: /api/gallery/statmech/partition_functions/images/thumb/sphx_glr_plot_02_equipartition_ladder_thumb.png
